TargaScript String Operations
============================

1. String Concatenation:
  Base string: Hello
  Concatenating different types with strings:
    String + String: Hello World
    String + Integer: Hello 42
    String + Float: Hello 3.14
    String + Boolean: Hello true
    String + Array: Hello [1, 2, 3]
    String + Object: Hello {"name": "TargaScript"}

2. String Properties:
  Original string: Hello, TargaScript!
  Length: 19

3. String Methods:
  Transformation methods:
    message.upper(): HELLO, TARGASCRIPT!
    message.lower(): hello, targascript!
  Trimming methods:
    Original padded string: "  spaced content  "
    padded.trim(): "spaced content"
  Replacement:
    message.replace("Hello", "Hi"): Hi, TargaScript!

4. Working with Substrings:
  Original CSV string: apple,banana,cherry,date,elderberry
  Splitting string:
    csv_data.split(","): ["apple", "banana", "cherry", "date", "elderberry"]
  Joining array back to string:
    fruits.join(" | "): apple | banana | cherry | date | elderberry

5. String Formatting with printf:
  Basic formatting:
  Integer: 42, Float: 3.141590, String: Hello

  Number formatting:
  Default float: 3.141590

  Rounded to 2 decimal places: 3.14

  Padded integer:    42

  Zero-padded integer: 00042

  Width and alignment:
  Right-aligned:      right

  Left-aligned: left      

  Multiple values:
  Alice is 30 years old


6. Complex String Operations:
  Formatting data in columns:
  Name       | Age | City
  -----------|-----|------------
  Alice      |  30 | New York  

  Bob        |  25 | Boston    

  Charlie    |  35 | Chicago   

  Creating formatted strings with variables:
  Product: Laptop, Price: $999.99, Status: In Stock


7. Practical Examples:
  Email validation (simple check):
    Email: user@example.com
    Contains @ and . in correct order? true
  URL parsing:
    URL: https://www.example.com/path?param=value
    Protocol: https
    Domain: www.example.com
    Path: /path?param=value

String operations demonstration completed!
